  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge · Sourcing</span>
    <h1>How to source OEM batteries from China: the complete guide</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-10 · Sources: IATF 16949, USTR Section 301, EU Batteries Regulation (EU) 2023/1542, IMDG Code</p>
    <p class="lead">Sourcing OEM batteries from China is not one decision — it is seven, and they happen in a fixed order. Get the order right and the process is mechanical; get it wrong and you lose a container’s margin to a supplier that never should have been shortlisted. This guide walks the sequence from specification to landed goods, gives you a weighted scorecard for choosing a factory, and ends with the parts suppliers would rather you skip: how to verify a factory is real, and what the landed-cost math actually looks like.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Source OEM batteries in seven stages — fix the specification, build a shortlist, verify the suppliers, audit the factory, issue a clean RFQ, confirm MOQ and lead time, and clear import compliance — in that order. The single highest-value step is verification: a trading company posing as a factory typically adds 5–15% and removes your quality control, so check the business licence before you pay. A first order is usually one 20ft container (600–800 units), lead time runs 30–45 days, and the whole cycle from RFQ to landed goods takes 60–90 days.
    </div>

    <h2>The seven-stage sequence</h2>
    <p>Every sourcing failure traces back to doing a later stage before an earlier one — quoting before the specification is fixed, auditing before the shortlist is credible, or negotiating MOQ before the supplier is verified. The stages are:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Specify</strong> — fix standard family, case group, Ah, CCA basis, terminal and technology.</li>
      <li><strong>Shortlist</strong> — pick 3–5 candidates from the right industry regions.</li>
      <li><strong>Verify</strong> — confirm each is a real factory with current certificates.</li>
      <li><strong>Audit</strong> — confirm process control and repeatability, on site or by third party.</li>
      <li><strong>RFQ</strong> — issue one complete scope so quotes are comparable.</li>
      <li><strong>Commit</strong> — confirm MOQ, lead time, payment and warranty.</li>
      <li><strong>Import</strong> — clear tariffs, certifications and dangerous-goods shipping.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Skip one stage and you pay for it later. A supplier you did not verify will surface a problem exactly when it is most expensive to fix — after the deposit is paid.</p>

    <h2>The decision tree: what to ask at each stage</h2>
    <p>Each stage has one question that matters more than the rest. Ask it, get a verifiable answer, then move on.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>The one question</th><th>What a good answer looks like</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1. Specify</td><td>Which standard family and CCA test basis?</td><td>“JIS D26, 72 Ah, 490 CCA at −15°C.”</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2. Shortlist</td><td>Which region and what is your real output?</td><td>Named plant location + daily output you can verify.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3. Verify</td><td>Show me your business licence and current IATF certificate.</td><td>Documents that check out against official registers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4. Audit</td><td>What is your plate curing window?</td><td>A stated window (typically 2–4 days) plus batch records.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5. RFQ</td><td>Quote one scope, one model per line.</td><td>A line-item quote that repeats your specification back.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6. Commit</td><td>What is the MOQ logic and lead time breakdown?</td><td>Container-based MOQ + production/shipping split.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>7. Import</td><td>Which certifications does my destination market require?</td><td>A named certificate set for your market.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Stage 1 — Fix the specification first</h2>
    <p>A battery that is not fully specified cannot be quoted, compared or audited. The five parts — standard family (JIS / DIN-EN / BCI / AS / SA), case group, capacity at C20, CCA on a named test basis, and terminal type plus polarity — are one decision, not five separate ones. A supplier that quotes “a 72 Ah battery” without asking the standard is guessing, and so is the price.</p>
    <p>The full treatment lives in the specifications cluster — start with the <a href="/knowledge/battery-specifications-guide/">automotive battery specifications reference</a> and lock the model against the <a href="/knowledge/battery-group-size-chart/">group size chart</a> before you contact anyone.</p>

    <h2>Stage 2 — Build a shortlist from the right regions</h2>
    <p>China’s lead-acid battery production is clustered, not spread evenly. Different regions specialise in different products and price points, and a buyer who shortlists by region gets a tighter set of candidates than one who shortlists by search ranking. The main clusters — Hebei (where {MANUFACTURER.shortName} is based, in Jinzhou), Shandong, Jiangsu, Zhejiang and Anhui — differ in process strength, export experience and cost structure. Read the <a href="/knowledge/china-battery-industry-regions/">China battery industry regions</a> guide before you build the list.</p>
    <p>Keep the shortlist to 3–5 factories. More than that and the RFQ comparison becomes unmanageable; fewer than three and you have no competitive reference.</p>

    <h2>Stage 3 — Verify the supplier is what it claims</h2>
    <p>This is the stage buyers skip, and it is the most expensive stage to skip. Three things must be true before a factory earns a spot on your list:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>It is a real legal entity</strong> — check the business licence and unified social credit code against the National Enterprise Credit Information Publicity System (<a href="https://www.gsxt.gov.cn/index.html">gsxt.gov.cn</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>It is a factory, not a trading company</strong> — the licence lists the business scope; “production” (生产制造) versus “trading” (贸易) is written on it.</li>
      <li><strong>Its certificates are current</strong> — an IATF 16949 certificate has a validity period and a scope; ask for the certificate number and confirm it against the certifier’s register.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The full method — including how to test whether claimed capacity is real — is in the <a href="/knowledge/battery-supplier-verification/">supplier verification</a> guide. The short version: a supplier that will not show its licence before an order will show you nothing useful after it.</p>

    <h2>Stage 4 — Audit the factory</h2>
    <p>Verification confirms the supplier is real; the audit confirms it can repeat your specification. Walk the line in the order the battery is made — grid casting, paste mixing, pasting, curing, assembly, formation, sealing, test — and at each step ask what is measured and how rejects are handled. The single most telling question is the curing window: a factory that rushes plate curing to save days ships batteries that fail early.</p>
    <p>For lead-acid the audit is lighter than for lithium — no cell-safety or thermal-runaway review — which makes it cheap to do properly. See the <a href="/knowledge/how-audit-battery-factory/">factory audit guide</a> for the full walkthrough, and run every candidate through the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/">supplier checklist</a> so they are scored the same way.</p>

    <h2>Stage 5 — Issue one complete RFQ</h2>
    <p>Most RFQs fail on three missing fields: the CCA test basis, the annual volume, and one standard per line. Without them, every supplier quotes a different scope and the prices are not comparable. Send the specification block, the volume block, the branding block and the commercial block as one document, one model per line. The <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-rfq-guide/">RFQ guide</a> has the template and the field checklist.</p>
    <p class="note">Do not put a price target in a first RFQ. Suppliers quote to a number when you give them one; quote on scope first and negotiate price second.</p>

    <h2>Stage 6 — Confirm MOQ and lead time</h2>
    <p>Battery MOQ is a container-logic number, not a marketing number. A 20ft container holds roughly 33 m³ and about 28,000 kg of payload, but the practical load for starter batteries is set by pallet stacking and carton crush strength — which is why a first order lands around 600–800 units rather than the 1,400+ the raw weight limit would allow. Lead time of 30–45 days is the sum of production plus pre-shipment testing, and it is stable year-round because lead-acid has no harvest season. The <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-moq-lead-time/">MOQ and lead time guide</a> breaks both down.</p>

    <h2>Stage 7 — Clear the import path</h2>
    <p>Before you commit a cent, know the destination market’s tariff, certification and environmental requirements. The US adds a 25% Section 301 surcharge on Chinese batteries on top of the base duty; the EU applies the <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/1542/oj">Batteries Regulation (EU) 2023/1542</a> and CE marking; Gulf states require SASO/GSO conformity; and lead-acid ships as Class 8 corrosive dangerous goods (UN 2794 / UN 2800) under the <a href="https://www.imo.org/">IMDG Code</a>. The <a href="/knowledge/battery-import-regulations/">import regulations guide</a> maps the requirements market by market, and the <a href="/knowledge/battery-shipping-warranty/">shipping and warranty guide</a> covers the dangerous-goods paperwork.</p>

    <h2>The weighted supplier scorecard</h2>
    <p>Score every shortlisted factory against the same five dimensions. The weights below are a starting point — raise the repeatability weight if you plan a multi-year program, and raise the certification weight if your market requires IATF 16949 or SASO.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Weight</th><th>What you are scoring</th><th>Where to find the data</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Specification coverage</td><td>25%</td><td>Can they produce your standard family and models in-house?</td><td>RFQ response + model list</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Certifications</td><td>20%</td><td>Are current IATF 16949 / ISO 9001 / market certs verifiable?</td><td>Certificate register</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scale & capacity</td><td>20%</td><td>Does daily output map to your annual volume without strain?</td><td>Factory audit</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Repeatability</td><td>20%</td><td>Is there a locked specification and change control?</td><td>Contract + sample history</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Export experience</td><td>15%</td><td>Which markets do they serve, and can they document them?</td><td>Reference + docs</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Score each dimension 1–5, multiply by the weight, and total. A score below 3.5 overall, or below 3 on repeatability, should remove a candidate regardless of price. {MANUFACTURER.name} holds {CERTIFICATIONS.join(', ')}, operates {MANUFACTURER.productionLines} producing {MANUFACTURER.dailyOutput}, and exports to {MANUFACTURER.exportMarkets} — so it can be scored with exactly this card. Run the detailed method in the <a href="/knowledge/how-choose-oem-battery-manufacturer/">how to choose a manufacturer</a> guide.</p>

    <h2>Two calculations that reframe the decision</h2>
    <p><strong>The audit-cost ratio.</strong> A one-day third-party audit in China costs roughly US$1,500–3,000. A first container of 700 batteries at a US$40 landed unit cost is US$28,000; a 12-month program at one container a month is over US$300,000. The audit is therefore under 1% of a single container and a rounding error against a year. Paying US$3,000 to avoid a specification drift that ruins a US$28,000 shipment is not a cost — it is the cheapest line item in the whole program.</p>
    <p><strong>The trading-company margin.</strong> A trading company that fronts as a factory typically adds 5–15% to the ex-factory price and removes your visibility into quality. On a US$28,000 container that is US$1,400–4,200 per shipment — more than the audit costs — and you have traded it for a supplier you cannot hold accountable on process. Verify before you pay, and the margin question answers itself.</p>

    <h2>What suppliers will not volunteer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>“Factory” photos can be bought.</strong> A trading company can photograph someone else’s line. The licence and a live walkthrough, not a photo set, establish who owns the equipment.</li>
      <li><strong>Certificates can be expired or suspended.</strong> A certificate is a point-in-time document. Ask for the number and check the certifier’s register — a suspended IATF certificate is not worth the paper.</li>
      <li><strong>A low unit price hides a hidden cost.</strong> A quote that skips the CCA basis, tooling or packaging is not a cheaper battery — it is a smaller scope. Compare on scope, not on the bare number.</li>
      <li><strong>MOQ reflects the supplier’s inventory pressure, not yours.</strong> A supplier that pushes a huge MOQ may be offloading stock, not protecting your unit cost. Ask for the MOQ logic.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Common mistakes that cost a container</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Quoting before specifying</strong> — you collect prices for three different batteries and call it a comparison.</li>
      <li><strong>Skipping verification</strong> — the cheapest stage to skip and the most expensive mistake to discover.</li>
      <li><strong>Buying on price alone</strong> — a 5% cheaper quote that drifts on specification costs more than it saves.</li>
      <li><strong>One model per RFQ line, ignored</strong> — mixed lines force a guess and a range instead of a quote.</li>
      <li><strong>Discovering import duties after ordering</strong> — a 25% surcharge found late can wipe the entire margin.</li>
    </ol>

