    <h2>The sourcing timeline, week by week</h2>
    <p>The 60&ndash;90 day cycle breaks into a predictable calendar. Here is the realistic schedule, with the stages mapped to weeks:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Weeks</th><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1&ndash;2</td><td>Specify + shortlist</td><td>Lock the 5-part specification; build a 3&ndash;5 factory shortlist from the right regions.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2&ndash;3</td><td>Verify</td><td>Check licences, certificates and capacity; drop the trading companies.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3&ndash;4</td><td>Audit</td><td>Walk the line or commission a third-party audit; confirm repeatability.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4&ndash;6</td><td>RFQ + sampling</td><td>Issue one RFQ; approve the pre-production sample.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6&ndash;10</td><td>Production</td><td>30&ndash;45 days of casting through formation and sealing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10&ndash;14</td><td>Shipping + clearance</td><td>3&ndash;5 weeks of ocean freight, customs and inland delivery.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The two places the schedule slips are sampling (because the specification was not locked first) and clearance (because the certification was discovered late). Both are preventable at the front of the sequence, which is why stages 1 and 7 deserve as much attention as the production weeks in between.</p>

    <h2>Payment terms, and the risk they carry</h2>
    <p>The standard OEM payment is T/T &mdash; 30% deposit, 70% before shipment &mdash; or a letter of credit at sight. The structure exists to split the risk: the deposit funds raw materials, and the balance transfers only when the goods are ready. Three things to negotiate explicitly:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The deposit size</strong> &mdash; 30% is standard; a supplier demanding 50% up front is asking you to carry more of its working capital.</li>
      <li><strong>The balance trigger</strong> &mdash; confirm the 70% is due against a pre-shipment inspection report and shipping documents, not just an invoice.</li>
      <li><strong>Escrow or L/C for first orders</strong> &mdash; a letter of credit at sight puts a bank in the middle for a first order with an unfamiliar supplier.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Payment terms are a verification tool as much as a cost. A supplier that will not accept a standard 30/70 split or an L/C at sight on a first order is signalling a risk it does not want you to see.</p>

    <h2>Sampling and the golden sample</h2>
    <p>Before mass production you approve a pre-production sample. That sample becomes the golden sample &mdash; the physical reference every later batch is checked against. Three disciplines make it worth anything:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Approve on the tested sample, not the photo.</strong> Ask for the CCA and capacity test report of the actual sample, on the named test basis.</li>
      <li><strong>Lock the golden sample in the contract.</strong> The purchase order should reference the sample serial and its test report, so repeat orders have a physical anchor.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep one sample yourself.</strong> You cannot adjudicate a future claim against a sample you never held.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>A supplier that rushes you past sampling into production is usually hiding the gap between its sample line and its production line. The <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-moq-lead-time/">MOQ and lead time guide</a> covers how sampling adds to the timeline.</p>

    <h2>How to structure a first order: pilot, then scale</h2>
    <p>Do not put your entire annual volume on the first purchase order. Structure it in three steps:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Pilot</strong> &mdash; a small batch (or a portion of a shared container) to validate the sample and the process.</li>
      <li><strong>First container</strong> &mdash; a full 20ft load, checked against the golden sample on arrival.</li>
      <li><strong>Scale</strong> &mdash; a rolling forecast once the first container passes inspection in your market.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The pilot costs a bit more per unit, but it buys you the evidence to scale with confidence &mdash; and it is far cheaper than discovering a drift across a year of containers.</p>

    <h2>Trading company or factory: how to tell in five minutes</h2>
    <p>Five checks separate a factory from a trader fronting as one:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Factory</th><th>Trader</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Business scope</td><td>Production (生产制造) on the licence</td><td>Trading (贸易) only</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Video call</td><td>Walks the line live, on request</td><td>Shows stock photos, deflects</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Technical depth</td><td>Names the curing window, alloy, CCA method</td><td>Repeats the brochure</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sample control</td><td>Sends a production-run sample with test report</td><td>Sends whatever it can buy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Price</td><td>Ex-factory, no hidden layer</td><td>5&ndash;15% on top of a real factory</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>None of these takes more than a day to run, and the licence check alone &mdash; free, on gsxt.gov.cn &mdash; eliminates most traders immediately. This is the highest-return five minutes in the whole sourcing process.</p>

    <h2>How to read a factory's export record</h2>
    <p>Export experience is the one scorecard dimension you can verify without visiting. Ask for three documents and read them for what they prove:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A recent bill of lading</strong> &mdash; proves they actually shipped to a named destination, not that they have a customer list.</li>
      <li><strong>Certificate of origin samples</strong> &mdash; shows which markets they routinely document, the paperwork half of compliance.</li>
      <li><strong>A customer reference</strong> &mdash; a real one you can call, ideally in your region, to ask the question you actually care about: did the 10th container match the first?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A supplier with 70+ destination markets and no ability to show a bill of lading is describing a wish, not a record.</p>

    <h2>A worked scorecard example</h2>
    <p>Here is the weighted card filled in for a hypothetical candidate, to show how the numbers behave:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Weight</th><th>Score (1&ndash;5)</th><th>Weighted</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Specification coverage</td><td>25%</td><td>4</td><td>1.00</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Certifications</td><td>20%</td><td>5</td><td>1.00</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scale and capacity</td><td>20%</td><td>4</td><td>0.80</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Repeatability</td><td>20%</td><td>3</td><td>0.60</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Export experience</td><td>15%</td><td>4</td><td>0.60</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Total: 4.00 &mdash; a pass, but note the repeatability score of 3 is at the floor. A candidate with this profile should be pressed on change control before volume is committed; a candidate scoring 5 on price but 2 on repeatability would total below the 3.5 cut-off and should be removed. The point of the weights is that they surface the weak dimension even when the headline score looks fine.</p>

    <h2>The pre-commitment checklist</h2>
    <p>Before you sign, all seven of these should be answered in writing:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The 5-part specification, confirmed and locked.</li>
      <li>The supplier verified as a factory, not a trader.</li>
      <li>The certificates checked as current.</li>
      <li>The audit completed, with the curing window confirmed.</li>
      <li>The RFQ returned on a single scope.</li>
      <li>MOQ, lead time, payment and warranty in the contract.</li>
      <li>The destination market's tariff and certification confirmed.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If any one is missing, the order is not ready &mdash; and the missing one is exactly where the margin will leak.</p>


